.TH SCENERENDER 1
.SH NAME
scenerender, scenedemo \- draw a scene as an image; drive a scene with a demo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scenerender
[
.B -w
.I width
] [
.B -h
.I height
] [
.B -t
.I time
] [
.B -v
.I view
] [
.B -F
.I font
] [
.B -n
] [
.B -l
]
.I dir
.br
.B scenerender
[
.I options
]
.B -r
.I recording
.PP
.B scenedemo
[
.B -n
.I ticks
] [
.B -d
.I ms
] [
.I scene
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Scenerender
draws a scene with the same renderer as the Matrix
.I scene-view
module and writes it to standard output in the Inferno image format
.RI ( image (6)),
which
.I present
displays and any file operation can copy.
No window system is needed.
.PP
The scene is a directory \(em a
.IR scenefs (4)
mount or plain stanza files \(em or, with
.BR -r ,
a recording such as
.BR /mnt/scene/history ,
replayed up to
.I time
(default: the end) with every entity's trail built from the whole
history.
The camera fits the data unless
.B -v
supplies a view line
.RB ( "center" " a b " "zoom" " z " "sel" " id " "follow" " id" ).
.B -n
leaves out the HUD,
.B -l
the default grid.
.PP
.I Scenedemo
writes a synthetic field survey \(em rovers mowing lanes, a relay, a
base and its radio range \(em as records to
.IB scene /log
(default
.BR /mnt/scene ),
one tick per second of scene time, every
.I ms
milliseconds (default 200; 0 as fast as possible), for
.I ticks
ticks (default: forever).
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
mount -c {scenefs} /mnt/scene
scenedemo -n 600 -d 0
scenerender /mnt/scene > /tmp/now.bit
cat /mnt/scene/history > /tmp/run.scene
scenerender -t 300 -v 'zoom -1 follow rover3' -r /tmp/run.scene > /tmp/t300.bit
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/scenerender.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/scenedemo.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR scenefs (4),
.IR scene (2),
.IR image (6)
